#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129278 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128856 :question/id #uuid "a1b2e13a-95cd-5e0f-aa97-c3e90433560c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12402" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R x+2y \\:\\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=5$ for $x\\geq 0,$ $y\\geq 0$ and between the surfaces $z=y$ and $z=y+3.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, \\mathrm{d}V = \n\\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta, z) \\:  r \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\, \\mathrm{d}r \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nz = z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/b7/b7b37ffe8e97ed4a661ee98750894641c6ed3354864dd5b54d2ed076e4fdb69b.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{5}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=y=r\\sin\\theta,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=y+3=r\\sin\\theta + 3.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta,z) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq r \\leq \\sqrt{5}, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\: r\\sin\\theta \\leq z \\leq r\\sin\\theta + 3\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x+2y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )[z]_{z=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{z=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}3{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{r}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )]_{r=0}^{r=\\sqrt{5}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{(\\sqrt{5})}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}5\\sqrt{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{5}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =\\pi /2} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{5}(1-0)-10\\sqrt{5}(0-1) \\\\  & =15\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ff4534b-6798-5f33-9823-643705229080" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542431 :question-generator/id #uuid "6edc76bd-ad65-59af-b3e7-ddae1eac78b5" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186129278.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129518 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bd34198-353c-5d77-a4f3-b94aae48f5d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\sqrt{5}π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126609} {:answer/id #uuid "49514054-bbe2-5c52-9a41-dcbaab2d3878" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126610} {:answer/id #uuid "6a5c8b4c-ae34-547e-bbaf-d473c39d68e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126611} {:answer/id #uuid "6ff3ab5b-66ac-584e-92cb-c1dbfb7e4d16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\sqrt{2}π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126612} {:answer/id #uuid "7f4a8acc-25d6-5523-b17f-7de7fd3724d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126613}] :answer-field/id #uuid "67803ba5-1ff4-58f3-9657-95238a5eb88b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126608}] :question/id #uuid "172f6194-d227-5c4b-a475-2c378a3312df" :question/math-academy-id "q-161522" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n$∭_(R)\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}dV$\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^{2} + y^{2} = 4$ and between the surfaces $z = 0$ and $z = 12 - 4y$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129767 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82d872b9-e5fc-5f15-b8eb-5b759613a87d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128084} {:answer/id #uuid "8de98f31-f25c-52af-8ed3-322493a4b904" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128085} {:answer/id #uuid "9d04d8ec-a37f-5fbf-8f97-eeb21f27bf09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128086} {:answer/id #uuid "acd41a1a-c3d2-5703-b896-208cee4a5ded" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128087} {:answer/id #uuid "0efff006-f396-5006-b66c-d98ac27a8a8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128088}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d042f8c3-3f5c-5724-9b36-795b04d59a4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128083}] :question/id #uuid "c05e88d4-386c-59e5-8052-d6f2602a779c" :question/math-academy-id "q-40785" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n$∭_(R)2zdV$\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^{2} + y^{2} = 9$ for $y \\ge 0$ and between the surfaces $z =-x$ and $z = 2 + x$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367687 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4499809-3e69-56fd-ae2d-6a7be6a2f313" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367689} {:answer/id #uuid "7a18ee38-c55e-5926-919e-7253e64d84cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367690} {:answer/id #uuid "c19e96fe-213d-5aee-87a7-b95114cd6cba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367691} {:answer/id #uuid "f49b664c-9ee3-5e83-95f3-68b1d4d79f04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367692} {:answer/id #uuid "7b775fa2-173e-5bee-83ea-f5deea8c16d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f49b664c-9ee3-5e83-95f3-68b1d4d79f04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367692} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4cda9b6-1f80-513a-bd23-5b6d6b230a0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367688}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f292eb6d-71c7-5c2c-9202-171aba30928c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161615" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3$ for $x\\ge 0$ and between the surfaces $z=y$ and $z=y+4.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/e2/e289cfbd210112a619018a7b905f7f2b1a9859ecb84b2517dd745705624d79d7.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=y+4=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le z\\le r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [z]_{z=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{z=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}4{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}[{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{r=0}^{r=\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\sqrt{3})^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{\\theta =-\\pi /2}^{\\theta =\\pi /2} \\\\  & =9(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367694 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "276b1353-c5cc-563b-8a69-6738850d94db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367696} {:answer/id #uuid "126208a2-9813-526b-b827-e8311e0cb6c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367697} {:answer/id #uuid "209dc6df-d901-5583-abd3-2fad14e0dcc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367698} {:answer/id #uuid "5cea0ee1-2b5f-51ce-8374-9a4bd836760e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367699} {:answer/id #uuid "4c607e3a-714e-5013-a85c-5369d31f2c47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cea0ee1-2b5f-51ce-8374-9a4bd836760e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367699} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0c7111c-c30f-53e3-ad02-5b0477136519" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367695}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ac483c0-6f15-5b71-8dbf-a2dbe49f14cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-161617" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=\\sqrt{9-({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/9c/9c4ea0ba92e19a201e3944defa6deb0bc1ee76971adaa809521722b6ac31241f.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{9-({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}=\\sqrt{9-{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}=\\sqrt{9-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{9-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }}\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{9-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}}z\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=\\sqrt{9-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{2}{(\\sqrt{9-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{2}(9-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{9}{2}r-\\frac{1}{6}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )]}_{r=0}^{r=3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{9}{2}(3)-\\frac{1}{6}(3)^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{27}{2}-\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[\\frac{27}{2}\\theta -\\frac{9}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )]}_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{9}{4}(0-0) \\\\  & =27\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367701 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "414072e0-1148-5168-96a6-11ffceca75b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367703} {:answer/id #uuid "5b6a336b-706c-5271-bea3-af543482e55d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367704} {:answer/id #uuid "e323b78c-c8f5-5407-aa4c-c6f8ca71ca46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367705} {:answer/id #uuid "9e58224d-5306-5bd6-bd6f-477dd4c4803f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367706} {:answer/id #uuid "bd911543-a08f-5fe3-9053-de56b1052391" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "414072e0-1148-5168-96a6-11ffceca75b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367703} :answer-field/id #uuid "698124ee-79ce-58d1-b542-2529ea23bae8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367702}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f2e3ad27-f613-58e0-9134-a9162e7320ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-161618" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and between the surfaces $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and $z=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/f4/f4c6f3acefa5d89fd5064de469952da3d68d379559bb4a6a4990a2ea24e4f861.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by  $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }=r,$  and\n\n- bounded above by $z=1.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,r\\le z\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}({(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2})\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{r}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}[z]_{z=r}^{z=1}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}(1-r)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}-{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}-\\frac{1}{5}{r}^{5}]}_{r=0}^{r=1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{20}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{20}\\theta ]}_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367708 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f2b3082-6cb8-5914-9afe-f85a3adcbf58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367710} {:answer/id #uuid "a3b80445-eff2-5ab0-bfc1-05a049baa216" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367711} {:answer/id #uuid "101c3407-a841-5c34-8633-d1920948c652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367712} {:answer/id #uuid "1f783130-9b38-5172-9806-a4bd1adada8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367713} {:answer/id #uuid "58c6062d-5eb5-5816-a9b2-85be967bca9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "101c3407-a841-5c34-8633-d1920948c652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367712} :answer-field/id #uuid "029f4078-a122-5f50-b918-a3671964a23e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367709}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fe694d83-40f2-521d-be86-5b8b77c0b5dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-161619" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2xz\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ for $y\\ge 0$ and between the surfaces $z=-x$ and $z=x+2.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/f8/f837d76caa5a6fd2c2de9a628a77c211fc12be6733fec4f1d3332b9c8ff0991c.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-x=-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=x+2=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta <\\pi ,\\,-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le z\\le r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2xz\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}2z\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2}2z{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [{z}^{2}]_{z=-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{z=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ({(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2)}^{2}-{(-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2})\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4-{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}4{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }[{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\frac{4}{3}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{r=0}^{r=1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))+\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]}_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi -0)+\\frac{1}{4}(0-0)+\\frac{4}{3}(0-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367715 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48f91aaf-23f4-58cc-bb6e-79abbf973ad2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367717} {:answer/id #uuid "0110e8ae-8c84-5ec9-96ac-09d9d4186528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367718} {:answer/id #uuid "433f6f39-f6a8-557b-94a7-8b8041dc09a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367719} {:answer/id #uuid "57d28256-683b-5487-bb1c-580dfa32cdbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367720} {:answer/id #uuid "ac2bcd9d-7761-5ddb-b551-270f06919e43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0110e8ae-8c84-5ec9-96ac-09d9d4186528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367718} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ccd0f6d-28f5-5082-93ab-14da8d3e3637" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367716}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b51de01d-0def-5c14-bcf2-79eb3ddf44e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-161620" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}4\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2$ for $y\\ge 0$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=6-2x.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/6f/6fe710e758ccda9a826d79f8219dc82b25f8cc39f65d92f02a6c28594c428a96.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=6-2x=6-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le 6-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}4\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}4\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{6-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }4r\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}4r[z]_{z=0}^{z=6-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}4r(6-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}24r-8{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{[12{r}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]}_{r=0}^{r=\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }12(\\sqrt{2})^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}(\\sqrt{2})^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }24-\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[24\\theta -\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]}_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =\\pi } \\\\  & =24(\\pi -0)-\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}}{3}(0-0) \\\\  & =24\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411857 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "012fa540-5e42-507c-b4d7-4be584a8a37c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411681} {:answer/id #uuid "6ca03990-4e3c-545a-89cf-a853738bd60a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411736} {:answer/id #uuid "74f3c49e-b5f3-5c5e-b0db-c523644cd561" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411740} {:answer/id #uuid "d2fa903f-cfe5-50db-9712-2e6a3f05be62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411796} {:answer/id #uuid "de2d26a9-566a-53ef-8878-c12a27c0ec9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74f3c49e-b5f3-5c5e-b0db-c523644cd561" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411740} :answer-field/id #uuid "f69e9c6d-9eea-5554-8872-d044f41b5762" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411849}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "47812564-3d50-5e9e-a030-d910fa667ee9" :question/math-academy-id "q-161606" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}4x\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ for $x\\ge 0,$ $y\\ge 0$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=y+3.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/b6/b6bf808437f48af8b0853108079bde0577c92812ebf868d04a0550ecacb3376e.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=y+3=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le z\\le r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}4x\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3}4{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [z]_{z=0}^{z=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +12{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +12{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{[\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +4{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]}_{r=0}^{r=2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +4(2)^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +32\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +32\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-4(-1-1)+32(1-0)) \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427903 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d3c3ffc-8f92-544d-a992-79894282f2ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427866} {:answer/id #uuid "68ea0354-f2c8-53b4-a86e-b9c1d0183a0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427873} {:answer/id #uuid "76bee58e-203b-522c-96c7-8110717e932b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427875} {:answer/id #uuid "f1a72efe-970d-57a6-bbba-8a4a895dd196" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427890} {:answer/id #uuid "fde9bf40-8671-597c-957b-0c2763e035e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d3c3ffc-8f92-544d-a992-79894282f2ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427866} :answer-field/id #uuid "13092669-e4dd-5fb1-923f-23241a6ac139" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427895}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8382ccc3-43fb-56df-8da1-731b85bcec1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161621" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3$ for $x\\ge 0$ and between the surfaces $z=x$ and $z=9+x.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/e2/e289cfbd210112a619018a7b905f7f2b1a9859ecb84b2517dd745705624d79d7.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=x+9=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +9.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le z\\le r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +9\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +9}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )[z]_{z=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{z=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +9}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +9-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}9{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}[3{r}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )]_{r=0}^{r=\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}3(\\sqrt{3})^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}9\\sqrt{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{3}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{\\theta =-\\pi /2}^{\\theta =\\pi /2} \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{3}(1-(-1))+9\\sqrt{3}(0-0) \\\\  & =18\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427904 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "230d0c4b-7bc5-5af6-8cf4-b180ffe873c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427867} {:answer/id #uuid "27240ef3-56dd-5f35-80d3-a273aa752805" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427868} {:answer/id #uuid "584cb703-7f97-5df9-a64a-8ecc7abca1af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427871} {:answer/id #uuid "f2487b1b-ed8a-59bd-8da7-a1715766603f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427892} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e86634-3513-5357-bbd9-f8582d0d5b2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8e86634-3513-5357-bbd9-f8582d0d5b2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427893} :answer-field/id #uuid "2673f4f3-d83e-5c77-94fc-178bcf193249" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427896}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ae4823a-479e-5870-b42a-306885da9109" :question/math-academy-id "q-161608" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=4-x.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/62/62abd56d2d374afb7c608aa64f701cfe94f096b823f964697a929c7c24f3a073.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=4-x=4-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}({(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2})\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}[z]_{z=0}^{z=4-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}(4-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}4{r}^{3}-{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[{r}^{4}-\\frac{1}{5}{r}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]}_{r=0}^{r=1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[\\theta -\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi } \\\\  & =(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{5}(0-0) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542375 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e084867-80ed-5e99-995e-d2f7c3a2775a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542396} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ca9be8-5d2e-57ab-a777-f3c7100f1c02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542405} {:answer/id #uuid "c0223973-2b8f-51d9-95d0-c1ac09f3ee98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542408} {:answer/id #uuid "cd2021fe-3967-595c-8aa9-57ea29061bf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542412} {:answer/id #uuid "e9530bbf-c5c3-5952-9005-276940846bcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9530bbf-c5c3-5952-9005-276940846bcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542423} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc2c7090-300d-5bba-8d56-8e59b2ef1c31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542420}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "267fd235-ecde-531c-b2aa-2c537397b3c6" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=1$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=3.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/c7/c7fe69ab2583c066df73bbf6719e4c58fea1ed565e345bc62c11b7ebc779508b.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=3=3.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le 1,\\ 0\\le\\theta<2\\cdot \\pi,\\ 0\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,dV &=\\iiint_\\Delta (\\sqrt{(r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))^2+(r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))^2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}\\int_{0}^{3}(r)^{2}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}(r)^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=3}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}(r)^{2}((3)-(0))\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}3\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}[(r)^{3}]_{r=0}^{r=1}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(((1))^{3})-(((0))^{3})\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(1)-(0)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}1\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=[\\theta]_{\\theta=0}^{\\theta=2\\cdot \\pi} \\\\ &=(2\\cdot \\pi)-(0) \\\\ &=2\\cdot \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542378 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ebd6dd6-7259-5120-97a3-66b51aeb5bdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542397} {:answer/id #uuid "ac6c862a-55e0-54f9-b7f6-7a2dc48c416c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1+3\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542401} {:answer/id #uuid "b062a522-6a46-5f79-9299-18183553eb22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542403} {:answer/id #uuid "f9945e02-9035-5167-8318-4361e517c387" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542428} {:answer/id #uuid "fc158f8d-2987-5499-a8ed-77f033124c1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc158f8d-2987-5499-a8ed-77f033124c1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542429} :answer-field/id #uuid "14d2c317-5999-5ae3-83ba-55833a642212" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542371}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3d825389-7a3e-5461-af4a-30fb0473dbe9" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=1$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=9-2\\cdot y.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/c7/c7fe69ab2583c066df73bbf6719e4c58fea1ed565e345bc62c11b7ebc779508b.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=9-2\\cdot y=-2\\cdot r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+9.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le 1,\\ 0\\le\\theta<2\\cdot \\pi,\\ 0\\le z\\le -2\\cdot r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+9\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,dV &=\\iiint_\\Delta (\\sqrt{(r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))^2+(r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))^2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}\\int_{0}^{-2\\cdot r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+9}(r)^{2}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}(r)^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=-2\\cdot r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+9}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}(r)^{2}((-2\\cdot r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+9)-(0))\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}-2\\cdot (r)^{3}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+9\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}[-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (r)^{4}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+3\\cdot (r)^{3}]_{r=0}^{r=1}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ((1))^{4}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))-(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ((0))^{4}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))+(3\\cdot ((1))^{3})-(3\\cdot ((0))^{3})\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))-(0)+(3)-(0)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}3-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=[3\\cdot \\theta+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)]_{\\theta=0}^{\\theta=2\\cdot \\pi} \\\\ &=(6\\cdot \\pi)-(0)+(\\frac{1}{2})-(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ &=6\\cdot \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542381 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f8d660b-d1ed-54c6-80fc-e62752d00c85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542370} {:answer/id #uuid "58a3a2b3-377a-5a2e-be8b-8d442f4d4cda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "256\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542383} {:answer/id #uuid "d370d519-dcf6-5dfa-93ca-e9d88e4f6b67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542414} {:answer/id #uuid "da860c21-af1f-52ef-9641-88927be2a946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "384\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542418} {:answer/id #uuid "ee4a4819-eeb8-5c8e-b89d-52d5af9200a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542425}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f8d660b-d1ed-54c6-80fc-e62752d00c85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542370} :answer-field/id #uuid "57e8e949-e40f-5a5f-a5fb-3d827a224266" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542382}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5434eee3-49f9-5e43-a178-4a79f52b75aa" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=16$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=3.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/ce/ce06d6892ad079a03c395b9ddac5db712d0b0e1797ec63c3c27f005c2287adb5.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=3=3.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le 4,\\ 0\\le\\theta<2\\cdot \\pi,\\ 0\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,dV &=\\iiint_\\Delta (\\sqrt{(r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))^2+(r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))^2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{4}\\int_{0}^{3}(r)^{2}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{4}(r)^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=3}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{4}(r)^{2}((3)-(0))\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{4}3\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}[(r)^{3}]_{r=0}^{r=4}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(((4))^{3})-(((0))^{3})\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(64)-(0)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}64\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=[64\\cdot \\theta]_{\\theta=0}^{\\theta=2\\cdot \\pi} \\\\ &=(128\\cdot \\pi)-(0) \\\\ &=128\\cdot \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542388 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6333ef2c-1cae-519e-a685-58c87c26695f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542384} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f4a2a2-bf74-5d31-bfe7-5cd3ea7de0d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542398} {:answer/id #uuid "c570990c-5907-5cdf-82b5-65a7360aed17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542410} {:answer/id #uuid "da65e013-dd31-5b49-8080-62718daaad6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542417} {:answer/id #uuid "f574aa17-b6bd-5157-b731-6ae70b45cf9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542427}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3f4a2a2-bf74-5d31-bfe7-5cd3ea7de0d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542398} :answer-field/id #uuid "99cc235e-b8c3-5f58-9f50-751a34cdf8ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542394}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "73587b1a-dcf1-5c8f-a281-fdffe625841b" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R 2\\cdot \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/8e/8ee9b491452a26902c4800a006c16f1bbca31f0e800ec9aad20dfeef68eed8f4.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=1=1.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le 3,\\ 0\\le\\theta<2\\cdot \\pi,\\ 0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R 2\\cdot \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,dV &=\\iiint_\\Delta (2\\cdot \\sqrt{(r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))^2+(r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))^2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{3}\\int_{0}^{1}2\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{3}2\\cdot (r)^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{3}2\\cdot (r)^{2}((1)-(0))\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{3}2\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}[\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (r)^{3}]_{r=0}^{r=3}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot ((3))^{3})-(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot ((0))^{3})\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(18)-(0)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}18\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=[18\\cdot \\theta]_{\\theta=0}^{\\theta=2\\cdot \\pi} \\\\ &=(36\\cdot \\pi)-(0) \\\\ &=36\\cdot \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542389 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "237c1dc0-9a08-5feb-9f93-69378beb0d5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "252\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542373} {:answer/id #uuid "646cbcae-95be-5a16-