through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{-(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}+14}=\\sqrt{-(r)^{2}\\cdot \\cos(2\\cdot \\theta)+14}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le 3,\\ 0\\le\\theta<2\\cdot \\pi,\\ 0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{-(r)^{2}\\cdot \\cos(2\\cdot \\theta)+14}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R 2\\cdot z\\cdot ((x)^{2}+(y)^{2})^{-\\frac{1}{2}}\\,dV &=\\iiint_\\Delta (2\\cdot \\frac{z}{\\sqrt{(r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))^2+(r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))^2}})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{3}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{-(r)^{2}\\cdot \\cos(2\\cdot \\theta)+14}}2\\cdot z\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{3}[(z)^{2}]_{z=0}^{z=\\sqrt{-(r)^{2}\\cdot \\cos(2\\cdot \\theta)+14}}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{3}(((\\sqrt{-(r)^{2}\\cdot \\cos(2\\cdot \\theta)+14}))^{2})-(((0))^{2})\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{3}-(r)^{2}\\cdot \\cos(2\\cdot \\theta)+14\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}[-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (r)^{3}\\cdot \\cos(2\\cdot \\theta)+14\\cdot r]_{r=0}^{r=3}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ((3))^{3}\\cdot \\cos(2\\cdot \\theta))-(-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ((0))^{3}\\cdot \\cos(2\\cdot \\theta))+(14\\cdot (3))-(14\\cdot (0))\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(-9\\cdot \\cos(2\\cdot \\theta))-(0)+(42)-(0)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}42-9\\cdot \\cos(2\\cdot \\theta)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=[42\\cdot \\theta-9\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)]_{\\theta=0}^{\\theta=2\\cdot \\pi} \\\\ &=(84\\cdot \\pi)-(0)+(0)-(0) \\\\ &=84\\cdot \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542392 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ef0905a-bdbf-5fea-9e69-8095deb48425" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{160}{3}\\cdot \\pi\\cdot \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542380} {:answer/id #uuid "91eb5f07-47ff-5d2d-ace1-01e291591fe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{80}{3}\\cdot \\pi\\cdot \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542393} {:answer/id #uuid "a951bc16-7847-5502-a4f0-fe6ba9cfffa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}\\cdot \\pi\\cdot \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542400} {:answer/id #uuid "db4ccc14-ea2a-521d-b14a-fa38d1435ac4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16+\\frac{40}{3}\\cdot \\pi\\cdot \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542419} {:answer/id #uuid "e84ef09f-1452-5309-a150-c450366b48f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91eb5f07-47ff-5d2d-ace1-01e291591fe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{80}{3}\\cdot \\pi\\cdot \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542393} :answer-field/id #uuid "de2b82c7-fc0a-58d0-a814-18a367a7b7ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542421}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89c3ded2-ece5-51bf-a7d4-4718cdf0a70d" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R 4\\cdot \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=2$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=5-2\\cdot y.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/c0/c015a0e708a8f931bc5d8857b69a734490518d79afb1f493183940f6d888ad1d.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=5-2\\cdot y=-2\\cdot r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+5.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{2},\\ 0\\le\\theta<2\\cdot \\pi,\\ 0\\le z\\le -2\\cdot r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+5\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R 4\\cdot \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,dV &=\\iiint_\\Delta (4\\cdot \\sqrt{(r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))^2+(r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))^2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{\\sqrt{2}}\\int_{0}^{-2\\cdot r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+5}4\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{\\sqrt{2}}4\\cdot (r)^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=-2\\cdot r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+5}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{\\sqrt{2}}4\\cdot (r)^{2}((-2\\cdot r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+5)-(0))\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{\\sqrt{2}}-8\\cdot (r)^{3}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+20\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}[-2\\cdot (r)^{4}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+\\frac{20}{3}\\cdot (r)^{3}]_{r=0}^{r=\\sqrt{2}}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(-2\\cdot ((\\sqrt{2}))^{4}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))-(-2\\cdot ((0))^{4}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))+(\\frac{20}{3}\\cdot ((\\sqrt{2}))^{3})-(\\frac{20}{3}\\cdot ((0))^{3})\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(-8\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))-(0)+(\\frac{40}{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{2})-(0)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}-8\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+\\frac{40}{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=[\\frac{40}{3}\\cdot \\theta\\cdot \\sqrt{2}+8\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)]_{\\theta=0}^{\\theta=2\\cdot \\pi} \\\\ &=(\\frac{80}{3}\\cdot \\pi\\cdot \\sqrt{2})-(0)+(8)-(8) \\\\ &=\\frac{80}{3}\\cdot \\pi\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542404 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a15b85a-ad02-5709-a268-9be5c6653c07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542369} {:answer/id #uuid "7b14ef7e-3f31-57b2-8f96-effeb4b7088d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72\\cdot \\pi\\cdot \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542390} {:answer/id #uuid "99fc5045-61f5-50fa-a0e2-8f0003e56fa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45\\cdot \\pi+200\\cdot \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542395} {:answer/id #uuid "ec96c562-7607-507f-9b9b-19e63a261a9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{8}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542424} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b901ef-66b6-5561-805a-3f02727212c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3b901ef-66b6-5561-805a-3f02727212c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542426} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b4a19c6-9237-53cb-b88d-eea0421ee991" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542372}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b6019cfe-9d9c-5e37-b6df-d53d3e14ef50" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R 2\\cdot x\\cdot z\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=6$ for $y\\ge 0$ and between the surfaces $z=-x$ and $z=x+10.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/04/0472caa4e40aac437429c5f542f8c7c155d69ff1a348e1d8a4c37b4ced9b472e.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{6}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-x=-r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta),$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=x+10=r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)+10.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{6},\\ 0\\le\\theta<\\pi,\\ -r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)\\le z\\le r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)+10\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R 2\\cdot x\\cdot z\\,dV &=\\iiint_\\Delta (2\\cdot z\\cdot r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_0^{\\sqrt{6}}\\int_{-r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)}^{r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)+10}2\\cdot z\\cdot (r)^{2}\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_0^{\\sqrt{6}}(r)^{2}\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)[(z)^2]_{z=-r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)}^{z=r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)+10}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_0^{\\sqrt{6}}(r)^{2}\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)((r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)+10)^2-(-r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))^2)\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_0^{\\sqrt{6}}(r)^{2}\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)((r)^{2}\\cdot (\\cos(\\theta))^{2}+20\\cdot r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)+100-((r)^{2}\\cdot (\\cos(\\theta))^{2}))\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_0^{\\sqrt{6}}20\\cdot (r)^{3}\\cdot (\\cos(\\theta))^{2}+100\\cdot (r)^{2}\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}[5\\cdot (r)^{4}\\cdot (\\cos(\\theta))^{2}+\\frac{100}{3}\\cdot (r)^{3}\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)]_{r=0}^{r=\\sqrt{6}}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}(5\\cdot ((\\sqrt{6}))^{4}\\cdot (\\cos(\\theta))^{2})-(5\\cdot ((0))^{4}\\cdot (\\cos(\\theta))^{2})+(\\frac{100}{3}\\cdot ((\\sqrt{6}))^{3}\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))-(\\frac{100}{3}\\cdot ((0))^{3}\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}(180\\cdot (\\cos(\\theta))^{2})-(0)+(200\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))-(0)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}180\\cdot (\\cos(\\theta))^{2}+200\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}180(\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\cos(2\\cdot \\theta)))+200\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}200\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)+90\\cdot \\cos(2\\cdot \\theta)+90\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=[90\\cdot \\theta+200\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+45\\cdot \\sin(2\\cdot \\theta)]_{\\theta=0}^{\\theta=\\pi} \\\\ &=(90\\cdot \\pi)-(0)+(0)-(0)+(0)-(0) \\\\ &=90\\cdot \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542409 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08dadd38-fb68-5779-a188-b784be4a6b2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542368} {:answer/id #uuid "6cbee069-7a87-51d0-9392-cbeec9ec586e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542387} {:answer/id #uuid "7d83eaae-f8b5-538e-af6a-fa57c9f45461" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542391} {:answer/id #uuid "a91f9201-835b-5ae6-be79-7518add5a8bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542399} {:answer/id #uuid "c9be48c8-ddc2-53db-874a-8450e1fe1768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9be48c8-ddc2-53db-874a-8450e1fe1768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542411} :answer-field/id #uuid "d30d0b97-29d6-5b4a-be3d-426bd7d15400" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542413}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0e834b8-fc6b-56b5-8146-fbd537f8a411" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R 4\\cdot \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=1$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=4.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/c7/c7fe69ab2583c066df73bbf6719e4c58fea1ed565e345bc62c11b7ebc779508b.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=4=4.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le 1,\\ 0\\le\\theta<2\\cdot \\pi,\\ 0\\le z\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R 4\\cdot \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,dV &=\\iiint_\\Delta (4\\cdot \\sqrt{(r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))^2+(r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))^2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}\\int_{0}^{4}4\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}4\\cdot (r)^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=4}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}4\\cdot (r)^{2}((4)-(0))\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}16\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}[\\frac{16}{3}\\cdot (r)^{3}]_{r=0}^{r=1}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(\\frac{16}{3}\\cdot ((1))^{3})-(\\frac{16}{3}\\cdot ((0))^{3})\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(\\frac{16}{3})-(0)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\frac{16}{3}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=[\\frac{16}{3}\\cdot \\theta]_{\\theta=0}^{\\theta=2\\cdot \\pi} \\\\ &=(\\frac{32}{3}\\cdot \\pi)-(0) \\\\ &=\\frac{32}{3}\\cdot \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542430 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24ddfd73-e817-5e19-bf8d-dd13eb09dba2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{128}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542374} {:answer/id #uuid "2b6f9f77-1552-57ae-884a-192b803d96da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542376} {:answer/id #uuid "3112b487-c350-57db-82e9-a721ed41fe97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542377} {:answer/id #uuid "ae54fd97-3a30-519e-9ebd-e1fb979dbe8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542402} {:answer/id #uuid "d4d4f8e7-781a-5b4c-827e-0040738832b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b6f9f77-1552-57ae-884a-192b803d96da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542376} :answer-field/id #uuid "b96cd23f-37b9-504c-a679-a848317b445b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542406}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ff5722e3-b420-50b2-8b72-a65be31820d3" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R 4\\cdot \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=4$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/05/0505715b5e78700e63d98fc2c1db8e7d4a3074b3e06f4202de1ce5a6831f7571.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=1=1.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le 2,\\ 0\\le\\theta<2\\cdot \\pi,\\ 0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R 4\\cdot \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,dV &=\\iiint_\\Delta (4\\cdot \\sqrt{(r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))^2+(r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))^2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{2}\\int_{0}^{1}4\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{2}4\\cdot (r)^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{2}4\\cdot (r)^{2}((1)-(0))\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{2}4\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}[\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot (r)^{3}]_{r=0}^{r=2}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot ((2))^{3})-(\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot ((0))^{3})\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(\\frac{32}{3})-(0)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\frac{32}{3}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=[\\frac{32}{3}\\cdot \\theta]_{\\theta=0}^{\\theta=2\\cdot \\pi} \\\\ &=(\\frac{64}{3}\\cdot \\pi)-(0) \\\\ &=\\frac{64}{3}\\cdot \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral by Converting to Cylindrical Coordinates"}]}
